aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/assets/3e2c56766f201095bee0eea101b877e30910c676c998f37d261fd7cf7aabb837.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-2)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,-6\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2a8c1d86158d0bce6141f5941bd280e4068ea6f9a97c0ea498503a64bc1f8988" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Develope